TASKS={
2:('Build a capacity worksheet from ten million daily users and explicitly assumed request, peak, and per-instance rates.','Use capacity() and compare 5787.04 average RPS, 28935.19 peak RPS, 81 active instances at the stated hypothetical capacity/utilization, and 82 for one-instance failure headroom. This arithmetic does not prove the database or complete architecture meets the workload.'),
3:('Implement a cold/warm request latency calculator with separate DNS, connection, TLS, application, and response stages.','Use the stated 20+40+40+55 = 155 ms cold example, then remove only stages actually reused. Do not subtract server execution because the connection is warm. Reject a negative stage time and keep the result labeled an assumed model, not a measured protocol guarantee.'),
4:('Implement the lost-counter interleaving and a bounded upload work queue.','Show A-read-zero, B-read-zero, A-write-one, B-write-one before repairing the critical section. Bound queued payload references and active workers separately. For I/O waiting, compare concurrency; for CPU work, account for runtime parallelism. An async keyword alone does not repair the race or add CPU capacity.'),
5:('Refactor a hardcoded payment gateway into an injected contract and a recording test implementation.','Test approved, declined, and uncertain outcomes without real charges. Keep integer units and currency explicit. A test fake validates orchestration only; real serialization, timeouts, and persistence need separate integration evidence. Do not map a timeout to decline simply to make a test pass.'),
6:('Build a small resolver that demonstrates transient, scoped, and singleton identities across two request scopes.','Within scope one, a scoped resolution returns A twice; scope two returns B twice. Transients return new identities; a process-owned singleton returns S in both scopes. Add a disposable resource and prove scope end cleans it up. Reject a singleton capturing a request-scoped disposable rather than hiding the bug in a locator.'),
7:('Divide shopping into catalog, orders, and payments modules and implement one boundary test.','Let orders request a payment through a contract; disallow direct imports of the payment storage adapter or writes to its private table. A test should enforce a meaningful dependency rule rather than every incidental class name. Show how a provider adapter can change while the order rule remains stable.'),
8:('Implement keyset pagination and a durable idempotency-key state machine over a synthetic payment operation.','Use keyset_page() for ordered pairs and test insertion before a page boundary. For idempotency, bind actor, key, fingerprint, state, and result atomically; reject a mismatched payload. A plain dictionary checked before a separate insert does not protect concurrent requests or restart recovery.'),
9:('Model profiles, transactions, messages, logs, and PDFs with their access patterns and ownership.','Justify each choice through point reads, ranges, invariants, retention, and byte size. Do not choose five engines merely to cover a taxonomy. Separate PDF bytes from metadata and preserve recoverable references; explain which derived stores can be rebuilt and which facts must remain authoritative.'),
10:('Run the last-seat claim using a conditional database update, then test conflicting writers.','Use claim_seat() against the same store and assert one owner wins while the other receives conflict. Explain which local transaction protects the invariant. Add rollback and crash histories; do not claim a local seat claim makes a remote payment part of the same transaction.'),
11:('Run the LRU fixture and reproduce a stale cache-aside population race.','At capacity three, A,B,C,A,D evicts B under ideal LRU. Then pause a reader holding version one, commit version two and invalidate, and resume the stale population. Version checks or another justified protocol must prevent an invalid freshness claim; a short TTL only changes exposure.'),
12:('Compare rendezvous owners before and after adding node D, and measure skew separately.','For many deterministic keys, a key either keeps its old owner or moves to the new owner in this model. That property does not guarantee balanced request traffic. Make one key extremely popular and show why adding cold owners cannot split that single-key pressure without a new serving strategy.'),
13:('Implement an input-versioned pipeline with a lineage record and a reindex generation switch.','Use Workflow to reject stale epochs and wrong input versions. Retain exact source and transform versions. Build a new index generation, account for concurrent changes and deletions, validate, and switch a serving pointer. Matching record counts alone is not sufficient evidence of correct versions or authorization.'),
14:('Evaluate logical clocks, vector relations, and fixed-quorum intersection for explicit histories.','lamport_receive(5,9) returns 10; vectors [1,0] and [0,1] are concurrent. For three fixed members, R=2,W=2 intersects. Explain why these calculations do not independently resolve concurrent business updates or prove linearizability. The missing protocol rules are part of the answer.'),
15:('Create a small fencing receiver and reject an old worker after a newer epoch has been accepted.','Use Workflow epoch checks for the local demonstration: epoch seven cannot complete after epoch eight owns the attempt. Explain why external storage must enforce its own fencing if old workers can still call it. A client-side lease flag cannot stop a resumed remote process.'),
16:('Run the outbox relay through publish-then-crash and process the duplicate with a local inbox transaction.','The example records two deliveries but one counter effect. Deduplication identity and effect commit share a transaction. If an external email or payment is substituted for the counter, the local inbox no longer establishes one external effect; use the provider contract and reconciliation.'),
17:('Model nested retries and write a provider-outage response policy.','Compute 3^4 = 81 potential attempts for four independently retrying layers. Introduce one end-to-end retry budget, bounded waits, admission, and channel isolation. Do not use a false payment approval as a fallback. Verify recovery behavior and unresolved business state after the provider returns.'),
18:('Test a tenant-scoped authorization predicate and all cross-tenant paths around it.','permit_demo() denies tenant A reading tenant B even when the action name is allowed. This synthetic predicate is not credential validation. Extend tests to cache keys, queued jobs, exports, and object capabilities; a correct predicate unused by a worker does not enforce anything.'),
19:('Build a trace waterfall and an end-to-end asynchronous completion indicator.','Use the five-second path with 4.6 seconds in pool acquisition. Separate wait from query execution and compare population evidence. Track submission-to-terminal job age rather than only HTTP acceptance. Avoid raw document content or high-cardinality request IDs as ordinary metric labels.'),
20:('Write an expand-and-contract migration and a mixed-version rollout test.','Add a compatible field, deploy readers that tolerate both versions, backfill under a bounded budget, verify, and remove old structure only after old readers retire. Test rollback before contraction and explicitly identify when rollback ceases to be safe. A canary must receive representative traffic.'),
21:('Compare three architectures using explicit fictional costs and equal requirement checks.','cost_options() totals 371, 551, and 1402 fictional monthly units. These are cost inputs only, not measured capacities or reliability. Compare each with original latency, failure, correctness, and operating constraints; reject an infeasible cheap option before ranking cost efficiency.')
}
